 we  find in the book of the way these  elections are being manipulated a great  one is from Madagascar where Marlo  Ravalomanana who's a very sort of famous  leader in the country a yogurt magnate  made his money in dairy he wanted to win  the election easily his opposition  leader was outside of the country he  decided that the best way to stop him  I'm actually competing in the election  was simply that every time he was flying  to the country keep in mind it's an  island he closed all of the airports  unilaterally right the guy never managed  to get into the country he couldn't  submit his papers he wasn't on the  ballot paper the election on the day was  pretty good quality why because you  don't need to worry about the election  on the day if you've excluded you're  most likely rival so on the day the  election was pretty free and fair what  happened international observers decided  that the election was good enough to get  a rubber stamp Ravalomanana not only  retained the presidency but with the  support of the international community  so my pitch here is the problem is not  so much democracy but that we have  allowed democracy to be hollowed out of  meaning and it's not just that this is  happening in far-flung tin-pot  dictatorships that you might think about  in other parts of the world  post-communist Europe sub-saharan Africa  Asia Latin America where recently for  example we could talk about the rigging  of the election in Venezuela it's not  just that democracy is being hollowed  out in those places it's also at risk of  being hollowed out in the United States  and the United Kingdom as well  the classic example in the United States  is it as many of you will know we have  seen massive processes of gerrymandering  whereby essentially leaders get to pick  who votes for them rather than the other  way around  in other words we see places in the  United States where we actually get  people who are able to actually select  the people voting for them by  distributing constituency boundaries in  that way and what that means is that we  actually see an awful lot of elections  both in the UK in the US that are  effectively uncompetitive excluding  large portions of the electorate from  the political process so my argument is  that we don't need to abandon democracy  but we need a process of democratic  renewal thank you and brilliantly on  time  I'm gonna go to Natalie next and then  Yasmine okay well I'm going to begin  with a thesis that you might find a  little bit shocking Britain is not a  democracy in the last election  sixty-eight percent of votes didn't  count that's from the electoral reform  Society you can look at their report  twenty percent of people were voting  tactically voting for a party that they  didn't want but they thought that might  stop some other party they didn't want  more people don't have a free choice and  if you go back to the 2015 election the  Tories won a hundred percent of the  power with the support of twenty four  percent of eligible voters now where  perhaps we might have some conflict on  this panel and I perhaps have a little  bone to pick with Nik is that I think  it's really dangerous to say that  democracy is failing  and this is a very common narrative  actually was in Sheffield a few days ago  was just talking to an ordinary voter in  the street and he said to me oh well you  know after brick said after Trump I  really don't democracy it's just failed  and I may have slightly jumped down his  throat more than I should have and said  well let's try democracy before we give  up on it  Trump of course is another great example  Hillary Clinton won the popular vote by  any normal standard she won the election  and Trump ended up as president I was in  Sheffield doing a debate against the  American professor had written this book  called against democracy and his thesis  was basically you know it's failed so we  have to give up on it and we kind of  need a mirror topic meritocratic  oligarchy to decide things in other  words people like me should decide how  to run the country well I would say if  you look around the world if you look at  the most democratic countries actually  it's the Scandinavian countries that  abroad to the most democratic and  they're also by far by most people's  measures the best countries in the world  to live in the best working countries  and if you take the most extreme example  of all perhaps which is Iceland now  Iceland  population about the size of Sheffield's  population Iceland has a prime minister  a president 64 members of parliament it  also has about 50 local government areas  roughly comparable to our councils and  those areas are actually guaranteed  autonomy in the National Constitution  they have a written constitution there  might be something of a hint for us in  that there and they have control of  local transport local schools so I would  argue that actually what we need is  vastly more democracy in Britain we  don't have democracy now let's introduce  democracy and you know Marianne perhaps  we're going to disagree on this but I'm  also I suspect it might be controversial  with this audience but I'm also going to  defend absolutely the idea of  referendums good referendums well  conducted referendums not like the one  we had in 2016 but I believe in trusting  the people I believe in democracy and I  think we should try it around the world  and we shouldn't be talking about it  failing we should be saying  let's try it let's do it let's follow  the people who've made it work yes when  I kind of almost totally agree with what  an accept but I want to go a bit further  because I am really heartily sick of  being accused of being against democracy  against the will of the people every  time I open my mouth because and I do  completely disagree with Natalie on the  referendum point but we'll come to that  because one of the things that's  happened in this country and I don't  know why whether we stopped citizenship  education whether we didn't think enough  about it there's been free education  here since 1944 how was it possible for  so many people how is it possible for so  many people to be lied to so many times  in the name of democracy and that  worries me a lot actually right that how  are people convinced of things that were  paitent lee lies during the referendum  and often are during elections and also  not understanding demo  she's not your vote on one day every  five years which is then counted the  whole structure is a fragile structure  and I do agree that's been hollowed out  because if you look at democracy in that  way we are talking about Hitler being  elected we are talking about the Mugabe  form of democracy we're talking about  these councils all across Pakistan and  Afghanistan of men and women these days  who decide what will happen in a mini  form of referendum and they come up with  the worst most inhumane policies  possible and they say this was the will  of the people so I think we need to kind  of communicate better all of us that the  essential ingredients of a good working  democracy freedom to debate freedom to  change your mind not license to degrade  and and disempower and oppress not  majorities and majority nism I do not  say it majoritarianism majoritarianism  because really good democracies have  space and protection for minorities not  nativism this is my country now and fair  policies for the protection of the human  rights of everybody so it again in  Turkey one of my the countries I loved  most because I thought then look it's  possible to be a Muslim which I am and a  secular Democrat once upon a time he  used democracy to kill secular democracy  [Applause]

